= Office Supplies, Hardware, and Event Materials

== Modern Workplace Technology and Supplies

At Noice & Toit LLP, our professionals work seamlessly across physical corporate offices, client sites, remote hotel hubs, and approved home working environments. Having dependable equipment, reliable connectivity, and essential stationery is critical to delivering top-tier professional services.

However, equipment, event materials, and digital subscriptions represent ongoing balance-sheet assets and carry data privacy, licensing, and IT security implications. This chapter governs commercial invoices for goods and services: what may be bought out of pocket, up to what amount, and what the submission note must state. It is organized by what was purchased; where an invoice mixes categories, each line is evaluated under its own category.

Classify each line by what the item is, not merely by the project or event where it will be used. General-purpose stationery, consumables, tools, peripherals, and powered equipment remain governed by their own sections. An item is instead an event material when it is customized as a giveaway, sign, or display; rented as temporary venue staging; purpose-built as a temporary prop; or consumed only in operating event-specific demonstration gear. Buying an ordinary supply or device for a booth does not by itself make it an event material.

== Routine Office Supplies and Stationery

Whenever possible, order stationery, paper, toner, notebooks, and writing materials through our central Office Services portal. For emergency, travel-related, or incidental purchases:

#card-do("Purchase minor emergency supplies out of pocket")[
  Incidental stationery, pens, notebooks, sticky notes, tape, printer paper, cables, and similar consumables may be expensed out of pocket up to *€50.00 / \$60.00 / £45.00 per invoice*. Amounts above the cap are deducted. Plant care products are not incidental consumables and never fall under this cap; they belong to the Facilities exclusion below.
]

#card-dont("Stockpile office supplies through retail stores")[
  Do not purchase bulk quantities of printer paper, high-end desk organizers, fountain pens, or leather folios out of pocket. Submit a requisition to Facilities and Office Services instead.
]

== Computer Hardware and Digital Peripherals

All laptops, tablets, smartphones, and associated hardware must meet Noice & Toit Enterprise Information Security requirements. Complete end-user laptops, tablets, and phones are never purchased out of pocket; IT issues them. Other powered equipment and programmable electronic devices are evaluated under the tool and peripheral thresholds below. This includes prototyping boards, control modules, laboratory devices, audiovisual equipment, and equipment used to operate a demonstration.

#card-do("Expense routine peripherals under the threshold")[
  Peripherals, tools, powered equipment, and programmable modules with a unit price under *€100.00 / \$120.00 / £90.00* (USB drives, travel mice, cables, adapters, hubs, soldering irons, hand tools, chargers, prototyping boards, and control modules) are reimbursable with an itemized invoice. A replacement charger or adapter bought while traveling is reimbursable up to €90.00 / \$100.00 when the note states that the original failed.
]

#card-do("Cite the IT ticket for high-value peripherals")[
  Peripherals, tools, powered equipment, and programmable electronic devices with a unit price of *€100.00 / \$120.00 / £90.00 or more* (external monitors, mechanical keyboards, webcams, noise-canceling headsets, external SSDs, specialist test equipment, and audiovisual or laboratory equipment) are reimbursable only when the note cites the IT Helpdesk ticket number under which IT cleared the purchase. Without a ticket number, that line is deducted.
]

#card-dont("Expense home office furniture, decor, or plants")[
  Office chairs, standing desks, lamps, footrests, monitor arms, shelving, rugs, artwork, and any live or artificial plants (including plant rental, plant care, and "green wall" services) are Facilities budget items. Plant care covers both the service and the supplies it consumes: potting soil, fertilizer, plant food and tonics, foliar and neem oil sprays, pesticides, and pots or planters are deducted like the plants themselves, whatever their unit price. They are deducted from expense reports regardless of purpose or pre-approval. Check the People portal for the regional remote-work ergonomic stipend.
]

== Software Licenses, Cloud Infrastructure, and AI Services

Software tools, SaaS platforms, cloud environments, and generative AI APIs are increasingly utilized for research and productivity. Unvetted software presents legal, intellectual property, and client confidentiality risks.

#card-dont("Expense software subscriptions or AI model credits")[
  Software licenses, cloud compute credits (AWS, GCP, Azure), SaaS productivity subscriptions, app store purchases, and generative AI API credits are deducted in full. Request them through the IT Service Portal, where IT reviews data protection agreements and provisions enterprise access.
]

== Event, Marketing, and Workshop Materials

Conference booths, hackathons, client workshops, and onsite trainings routinely require materials that no central catalog stocks: booth displays, branded giveaways, demo props, signage, printed handouts, and consumables.

#card-do("Expense event materials for a named event with stated pre-approval")[
  Booth and display materials, promotional giveaways and team swag, workshop props and demonstration gear, signage, printing, and courier delivery of such items are reimbursable in full when the note (a) names the event, workshop, or campaign and (b) states that a named person in the responsible department pre-approved the purchase. A title is welcome but not required. Both statements are required; if either is missing, the event material lines are deducted.

  Event materials also include temporary staging and decor rented for the event venue, such as banners, backdrops, and rented plants, moss walls, and other greenery used as booth or stage decoration. This staging exception overrides the general plant exclusion in this chapter and in Chapter 11: greenery rented temporarily for a named, pre-approved event is an event material, while recurring office or home greenery remains a Facilities budget item.
]

#card-dont("Buy event materials that double as personal or office goods")[
  Furniture, decor, apparel for personal use, and powered or electronic equipment above the peripheral threshold do not become reimbursable by being bought for an event. They remain governed by their own sections above. The same holds for plants, with the sole exception of temporary staging greenery rented for a named, pre-approved event under the card above: monthly office plant rental, permanent planting, and plant care for office greenery are Facilities budget items regardless of purpose or pre-approval.
]

=== A Note on Rubber Ducks and Other Icons
The firm's tolerance for unusual event materials has a history. The 2021 Hamburg hackathon booth, staffed by a team that had ordered a crate of squeaky ducks as a load-testing mascot, generated more qualified leads than any booth before or since. The ducks are now something of a tradition, and Finance has stopped asking why. This does not mean that anything bought in bulk and painted yellow is an event material; it means that giveaways for a named, pre-approved event are.

== Team-Building Activities

Activities such as escape rooms, cooking classes, bowling, or go-karting for a team are reimbursable up to *€50.00 / \$60.00 / £45.00 per participant* when the note states the occasion, the headcount, and the pre-approving budget owner. The excess is deducted. Meals during such events follow Figure 2.

== Shipping, Courier, and Connectivity

- *Courier and postage* for client deliverables, trial bundles, or replacement equipment is reimbursable in full when the note states what was shipped.
- *Mobile data*: International roaming passes, travel eSIMs, and prepaid data bundles bought while traveling without a firm SIM are reimbursable up to *€50.00 / \$60.00 / £45.00 per trip*.

== Professional Publications and Books

- *Books and Technical Reference Manuals*: Treatises, handbooks, and reference volumes required for active client engagements may be expensed up to *€80.00 / \$90.00 / £70.00 per item* when the note cites the project or client matter. The excess per item is deducted.
- *Professional Periodicals*: Subscriptions to the Financial Times, Wall Street Journal, Economist, or specialized legal journals must be ordered through Global Library and Information Services and are deducted from expense reports.

== Ownership and Asset Tracking

Any piece of hardware, technical device, peripheral, or book purchased with firm funds or reimbursed through an expense report remains the exclusive property of Noice & Toit LLP:
- High-value hardware peripherals must be registered with the local office IT asset manager upon receipt.
- When leaving the firm, an employee must return all reimbursed equipment and devices alongside their firm-issued laptop and mobile phone.

== The Asset Lifecycle: Request to Disposal

Every item of firm hardware follows the same lifecycle, whether it is a laptop issued to a first-year associate or a Booth Duck of unusual provenance. The lifecycle has five phases: request, review, issuance, service, and disposal. The Asset Management Office owns the process end to end and publishes the current lifecycle map on the People portal. Understanding the lifecycle matters for expense purposes because each phase generates a record, and records are what Finance relies on when an invoice is queried.

A request begins either with a Helpdesk submission from an employee or with a replacement cycle triggered by the Asset Management Office. Requests are triaged by the local IT coordinator, who confirms that the item is not already sitting unopened in a cupboard two floors away. The firm discovered some years ago that it owned eleven label printers across three offices, a finding that now appears, without comment, in every onboarding deck.

During service, equipment is assigned to an employee, an office, or a project pool. Assigned equipment travels with its assignee, including to client sites, and remains the employee's responsibility until formally reassigned. Equipment left in a taxi, a conference lobby, or a client's reception area must be reported to the Helpdesk on discovery; the report is not a disciplinary matter, but the absence of one usually is.

Disposal is never self-service. Hardware leaving the firm — through end-of-life recycling, resale to staff under the annual equipment sale, or charitable donation — is wiped, deregistered, and signed off by the Asset Management Office. Employees do not dispose of firm hardware in household recycling, and hardware does not leave a desk for the skip; it leaves via the sealed disposal crates kept beside every office's printer alcove.

=== The Procurement and Security Review Workflow

Hardware and software purchases that exceed the out-of-pocket routes described above follow a single review workflow administered by the Technology Review Board, a standing body chaired by Information Security with members from IT Operations, Data Protection, and Procurement & Vendor Management. The workflow exists so that a purchase is examined once, thoroughly, by the right people — not repeatedly, superficially, and by whoever answers the Helpdesk phone that afternoon.

#table(
  table.header([*Stage*], [*Performed by*], [*What is examined*], [*Outcome*]),
  table.hline(),
  [*1. Intake*], [Requester, via the IT Service Portal], [Business justification, category, and anticipated assignment], [Request logged and routed],
  [*2. Technical review*], [IT Operations], [Compatibility, support burden, and overlap with existing estate], [Recommendation to proceed, consolidate, or decline],
  [*3. Security review*], [Information Security Office], [Attack surface, data exposure, and vendor security posture], [Clearance, clearance with conditions, or rejection],
  [*4. Data-protection review*], [Data Protection Officer], [Personal data flows, processor terms, and cross-border transfer], [Recorded assessment in the software register],
  [*5. Commercial check*], [Procurement & Vendor Management], [Existing contract coverage and supplier alternatives], [Order placed through the correct channel],
  [*6. Registration*], [Asset Management Office], [Asset details and assignment], [Entry in the asset register],
)

Requests move through the stages in order, and a request that fails one stage returns to the requester with written reasons rather than circulating informally in search of a second opinion. The Technology Review Board meets fortnightly; urgent requests can be cleared between meetings by the chair, who then reports the clearance at the next session. Requesters should not ask individual board members for informal sign-off at the coffee machine, a practice the board has described, in its minutes, as "the shadow workflow" and asked to be spared.

=== Software and Data-Protection Assessment

Software receives particular scrutiny because it can copy data out of the firm faster than any hardware in the building. Before any tool is provisioned, the Data Protection Officer records an assessment covering what data the tool touches, where that data is stored, who at the vendor can access it, and what happens to it when the firm stops paying. The assessment is filed in the software register alongside the license terms and the named internal owner.

Free tools are assessed like any other. The absence of an invoice does not signal the absence of risk; several of the firm's least favourite incidents began with a browser extension that somebody described, afterward, as "basically nothing." Generative AI services receive an additional layer of review covering what is transmitted to the model provider and whether client material may be processed at all. The current permitted-use matrix is published on the Information Security intranet page and updated as providers change their terms.

Tools that fail assessment are added to the blocked list, which is enforced at the network level. Requesting a blocked tool through the IT Service Portal in the hope of a different answer is not forbidden, but the request should present new facts — a different vendor plan, a changed data residency, a signed enterprise agreement — rather than a second copy of the first request with the word "urgent" attached.

