== Sustainable and Circular Procurement

The Sustainability Working Group reviews the equipment categories in this chapter for durability, repairability, and end-of-life options. Its standing guidance is unglamorous and effective: buy fewer things, buy things that last, and buy things that can be handed on. Preference is given to suppliers offering repair services, take-back schemes, or refurbished stock, and the circular alternatives are noted in the vendor directory reviews.

The firm's refurbished-laptop program is the quiet star of this section. Devices returned at the end of their first assignment cycle are wiped, re-imaged, and re-issued to teams whose needs are less demanding, extending useful life by years. The program's most persuasive argument, per the Working Group's last annual note, is that the refurbished machines statistically outperform their new siblings on reliability, presumably because they have already got their teething troubles over with.

Packaging and disposable materials for events fall under the same guidance. Reusable signage frames, washable booth banners, and hire rather than purchase of one-off display items are the default; the Sustainability Working Group publishes a short checklist on the People portal and answers questions without judgement, mostly.

== Inventory and the Asset Register

The asset register is the single authoritative record of what the firm owns, where it is, and who is responsible for it. It is maintained by the Asset Management Office and reconciled against physical stock checks, which each office hosts on a rotating schedule. The register's quality depends on the small acts of accuracy performed by everyone: registering a peripheral on receipt, recording a reassignment, and reporting a device that has been missing long enough to become furniture.

