The register exists to answer questions quickly: which laptops are due for replacement, how many spare headsets the Hamburg office actually holds, and who was last recorded as holding the projector that vanished during the 2022 offsite. Employees can view their own assigned equipment through the People portal and should raise discrepancies with the local IT coordinator rather than quietly living with them.

== Accessibility Equipment

The firm provides accessibility equipment — screen readers, ergonomic input devices, amplified headsets, document cameras, adjustable monitor arms, and comparable aids — through the IT Service Portal, with occupational health input where helpful. Such equipment is ordered centrally, issued promptly, and never expensed out of pocket, so that no one has to front the cost of a workplace accommodation and wait for a reimbursement conversation to go well.

Requests for accessibility equipment follow a shortened review path: Data Protection review is not required unless the aid processes personal data, and commercial checks are pre-cleared for the standard catalogue. The Accessibility Task Force, which includes employees who use the equipment daily, reviews the catalogue twice a year and retires items that technology has left behind. Managers are reminded that a request for accessibility equipment is an ordinary workplace request, handled with the same discretion and the same urgency as any other — which is to say, promptly and without commentary.

== Training on Secure Handling of Equipment

Information Security runs a short mandatory module on the secure handling of firm equipment, renewed annually. The module covers physical custody during travel, the reporting of loss, the dangers of borrowed charging cables in public places, and the correct use of the disposal crates. It is deliberately brief, because training that runs long is training that gets clicked through; the module's designers have treated brevity as a security control.

New joiners complete the module during onboarding and before receiving portable hardware. Existing staff complete it annually, and teams with recurring travel patterns can request a live refresher, which the security team delivers with a slide deck that has been described as unexpectedly funny. Completion is tracked, and uncompleted modules generate escalating reminders whose tone shifts, by the third reminder, from cordial to biblical.

#card-do("Report equipment loss the same day")[
  A lost or stolen device is reported to the Helpdesk immediately upon discovery, wherever in the world it happens. Same-day reporting protects the firm, the client, and the employee. The report is confidential and handled without blame; delay, not loss, is what turns an incident into a problem.
]

#card-dont("Route equipment through the expense system to dodge the process")[
  Buying hardware through the peripheral route when it should have gone through the review workflow, splitting an order to keep lines small, or asking a colleague to place the order are all treated as process circumvention. The equipment is registered retroactively, the requester has a conversation with Procurement & Vendor Management, and nobody enjoys it.
]

== Frequently Asked Questions

*Q: We bought 400 rubber ducks as giveaways for a hackathon booth. Seriously?*\
A: Seriously. Giveaways for a named event are event materials. If the note names the hackathon and states who pre-approved the budget, the ducks are reimbursed in full.

*Q: The invoice for our booth props also includes a rented ficus for the booth. What happens?*\
A: If the ficus is temporary staging rented for the named, pre-approved event, the staging exception above makes it an event material, and it is reimbursed with the props. Recurring office or conference-room greenery is not staging: that line is deducted, together with its share of VAT, and the invoice is partially approved.

*Q: The vendor invoice is addressed to a client company rather than to the firm. Is it still valid?*\
A: The buyer name on a commercial invoice does not affect reimbursability, provided the employee paid it and the note explains the purchase.
